{"items":[{"owner":{"account_id":9035670,"reputation":6136,"user_id":6731765,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/cc7f4e3138ee85f5f9c69da2d3f90a87?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"NS0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/6731765/ns0"},"is_accepted":false,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1507760326,"last_edit_date":1507760326,"creation_date":1507759976,"answer_id":46698365,"question_id":46698244,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<p>A few notes: </p>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Checking if <code>input[i]</code> it not in <code>Pan</code> should be done by using <code>not</code></li>\n<li><code>Valid==False</code> should be <code>Valid=False</code> because you're setting a variable</li>\n<li><code>For</code> should start with a lowercase</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Try using the following, I cleaned up the variables a little</p>\n\n<pre><code>pan = “1234567890-=[]{}%^*+_\\|~&lt;&gt;€$¥•.,?!/()£&amp;@“ # this is my list that I’m going to check an input against\n\nvalid = False\nwhile valid == False:\n     valid = True\n     forename = input(\"Please enter name\")\n     for i in range (0,len(pan)):\n         if input[i] not in pan:\n             valid = False\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>Here's a more concise version </p>\n\n<pre><code>name = input(\"Please enter a name\")\nvalid = True\nfor char in pan:\n    if char in name:\n        valid = False\n        break\n</code></pre>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":6136522,"reputation":78046,"user_id":4785185,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/ChR5h.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Prune","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/4785185/prune"},"is_accepted":true,"score":0,"last_activity_date":1507761618,"creation_date":1507761618,"answer_id":46698626,"question_id":46698244,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<p>You have several problems in your posted code.  For one thing, you require that the input name be made of characters in <strong>Pan</strong> -- names are usually alphabetic, so I'm worried that your logic is reversed.  However ...</p>\n\n<ol>\n<li>This code does not produce the given output: the misspelled \"for\" will draw an earlier error.</li>\n<li>You need to index the variable holding your input, not the input function.  That's the error you got.</li>\n<li>Learn to think in Boolean terms: any time you have \"== False\" or \"== True\", you're not doing this properly.</li>\n<li>Grab the input only once -- don't make the user enter it for every character check.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>Here's your code after these updates:</p>\n\n<pre><code>pan = “1234567890-=[]{}%^*+_\\|~&lt;&gt;€$¥•.,?!/()£&amp;@“  # valid characters\n\nforename = input(“Please enter name”)\n\nvalid = False\nwhile not valid:\n     valid = True       # input is good until we get a bad value\n     for i in range (0, len(forename)):\n         if forename[i] not in pan:\n             valid = False\n             break\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>Next, note that you never actually need the index of each character; you need only the characters.  user6731765's answer shows this update.</p>\n\n<p>For advanced shortening, learn the <em>all</em> and <em>any</em> functions; <em>all</em> works here to check the entire input string easily:</p>\n\n<pre><code> forename = \"INVALID NAME\"\n\n # Are all the name's characters found in pan?\n while not all(char in pan for char in forename):\n     forename = input(“Please enter name”)\n</code></pre>\n"}],"has_more":false,"quota_max":300,"quota_remaining":289}